# healthchecks.io dead-man's-switch ping URLs.
#
#   cp .af3cache-monitoring.env.example .af3cache-monitoring.env
#   chmod 600 .af3cache-monitoring.env
#
# Read by helpers/{backup,af3cache_backup,volume_guard,monitoring_watchdog}.sh,
# which locate this file relative to their own directory.
#
# WHY THESE EXIST: every other alert works by SENDING something, which cannot
# cover the case where the sender is what broke. A deleted cron line, a moved
# script, a wedged Prometheus, or a powered-off VM all produce silence -- and
# silence is indistinguishable from "all is well". These invert that: the checks
# alert when pings STOP. This is the layer that would have caught the two-month
# backup outage that "email me on failure" did not.
#
# Create four checks at https://healthchecks.io (free tier, 20 checks, no card):
#
#   af3cache-backup-daily           period 1d    grace 4h
#   af3cache-backup-weekly          period 7d    grace 12h
#   af3cache-volume-guard           period 10m   grace 20m
#   af3cache-monitoring-watchdog    period 10m   grace 20m
#
# Each yields a ping URL like https://hc-ping.com/<uuid>. Scripts append /fail
# on failure. An unset URL is skipped silently, so a missing value disables the
# switch WITHOUT any error -- verify with `bash -x <script>` and confirm a curl
# to hc-ping.com actually appears. Do not trust the exit code: these URLs are
# also a capability (anyone holding one can forge "all is well"), so treat this
# file as a secret and never commit it.
HC_BACKUP_DAILY_URL=
HC_BACKUP_WEEKLY_URL=
HC_VOLUME_URL=
HC_WATCHDOG_URL=
